  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 104 PM CDT Thu Oct 29 2026\n\nWeak ridging aloft moves through the area tonight into Friday. This \nwill allow for an increase in south to southeast flow in the lower \nlevels. Models soundings point towards some possible patchy fog \nagain later tonight, but stratus should increase from south to north \nlate tonight into Friday afternoon. So for now not anticipating \nwidespread fog issues. Other than the increasing stratus through the \nday, Friday should mostly be dry. By afternoon into the evening a \nwave will rotate northward bringing slightly cooler temperatures \naloft above the increasing low level moisture. This should bring \nsome weak instability with maybe 200-500 J/kg CAPE and no real shear \nto speak of. This the expectation will be for mainly scattered \nshowers with isolated thunderstorms. The better chances for showers \nand isolated thunderstorms should be near and east of I-29 with \nareas near and west of the James River possibly capped and seeing a \nbetter chance for drizzle with roughly the lowest 5000 feet \nsaturated and weak lift moving through. For now rainfall amounts \nlook light with some spotty tenth of an inch or so possible.\n\nSaturday the challenge will be trying to break through a fairly deep \nstratus layer as weak upper level support gradually diminishes. For \nnow expect cloud cover the linger and temperatures to not reach \ntheir full potential, likely topping out in the lower 60s in most \nlocations. Still mild for this time of year.\n\nSaturday night into Sunday expecting mainly dry conditions as \nsaturation aloft diminishes and any weak instability is gone. Sunday \nwill likely see a resurgence of stratus ahead of the next wave coming \nSunday night into Monday. This should again keep high temperatures \nmostly in the lower 60s.\n\nSunday night will see a surge of warm air advection in the lower \nlevels as low pressure deepens to the west southwest and an initial \nwave races by to the northeast. Would not be at all surprised if we \nsee enough instability Sunday night into Monday morning to produce \na few thunderstorms as the band of strong warm advection moves \nthrough. After this a few question marks on low pressure \ndevelopment. With the jet still pretty strong and southwest of the \nmain trough, expect low pressure to deepen across KS/NE area, then \nlift east or northeast into Tuesday. The low pressure movement is \nfairly low confidence as the models are yet to agree upon how much \njet energy wraps around to the east side of the low, but if enough \ndoes, we could see rainfall continue well into Tuesday. For now even \non the backside of this system it is a bit too warm for snow, so \nthinking all rain.\n\nIf this system were to wrap up as much as the ECMWF suggests, a few \nsnow showers would be possible Wednesday into Thursday, but not a \nlot of support from other models, so nothing more than something to \nkeep an eye on.\n\nAs for precipitation amounts, the latest GEFS is still bullish \nbringing over an inch of rainfall to many locations, with a 50-60 \npercent chance in far southeast SD, northwest IA and northeast NE. \nThe latest AI model information leaning towards the lower side on \nrainfall amounts which worked well with the rain event this past \nMonday when many models (lower and hi res) and ensembles were \nindicating a widespread 1-2.5\" of rain and we saw something closer \nto 0.50 to 1.25 inch rainfall amounts.",
